  "confidence": 0.42,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "short_to_supply",
      "components": ["Q1", "R1", "JP1", "U2"],
      "pins": ["Q1.gate", "R1.2", "JP1.3", "U2.8"],
      "nets": ["Q_PMS_GSD", "VCC_STABLE", "+5V"],
      "observed": "A wire (drawn diagonally across the sheet) runs from the left edge of the ATtiny symbol U2 – i.e. from its supply pin (U2 pin 8 / VCC, the net that also carries +5V) – directly into the high‑side PMOS gate node Q_PMS_GSD / VCC_STABLE, which is shared with R1.2 (the 10 k gate pull‑up to VCC), JP1 pin 3 and Q2's collector.",
      "expected": "The PMOS gate / VCC_STABLE enable net must be driven only through the level‑shifter (Q2 via R2) and pulled up by R1; it must never be hard‑tied to a supply or ground pin. The ATtiny has no GPIO on the left side of its symbol (only VCC/GND there), so this wire shorts a control net to a power rail.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "JP1 'VCC_STABLE' pin 3, R1 (10K) bottom and Q1 gate all on net Q_PMS_GSD, joined by a wire to U2 left‑side supply pin (sheet /, page 1)"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "U2 pin 8 = VCC with +5V power symbol on the left edge of the ATtiny45‑20P symbol (page 2, column 6)"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "an ATtiny‑driven fan controller (Atmel/ category)"}
      ],
      "impact": "The high‑side fan switch is no longer controllable: with the gate pinned to VCC the PMOS stays off (fan never runs), and R1's pull‑up is shorted to the rail; if the wire instead lands on the GND pin the PMOS is stuck on and the enable/PWM power control is lost.",
      "confidence": 0.4
    },
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": ["U1"],
      "pins": ["U1.1", "U1.2"],
      "nets": ["+5V", "VCC"],
      "observed": "U1 is an LM1117‑5.0 (5.0 V fixed‑output LDO): pin 1 (VI) is tied to +5V and pin 2 (VO) is tied to VCC, so the regulator's nominal output equals its input rail.",
      "expected": "A linear regulator requires its input to exceed its output by at least the dropout voltage; feeding a 5.0 V part from a 5 V rail cannot regulate, and the 'VCC_STABLE' fan rail it is meant to provide will sit at least one dropout below +5V and be unregulated.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "U1 LM1117‑5.0, pin 1 VI to +5V, pin 2 VO to VCC (page 2, column 6)"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "an ATtiny‑driven fan controller"}
      ],
      "impact": "The VCC rail is not a regulated/stable supply as the VCC_STABLE jumper/PMOS stage implies; the fan and the tach pull‑up (R6 to VCC) see a sagging, unregulated voltage.",
      "confidence": 0.35
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The schematic shows a wire shorting the high‑side PMOS gate / VCC_STABLE enable net to the ATtiny's supply pin, which would lock the fan power switch off (or on) and defeat the enable control, and a 5.0 V LDO fed from the 5 V rail that cannot regulate – both deviations from a sound ATtiny fan‑controller design."
}

